# open-ALERT base station image: when two disks carry the same identifier, the
# names that stand for it (/dev/disk/by-uuid/…, by-partuuid/…, by-label/…)
# point to the one that is not on USB.
#
# Linux is told its main partition by its identifier (root=UUID=… on the
# kernel's start-up line), and the start-up file system (initramfs) checks and
# mounts whatever /dev/disk/by-uuid/… points to at that moment. Every disk made
# from the image carries the same identifier, and among equals udev points the
# name to the disk it saw last: a USB stick made from the image, found a moment
# after the computer's own disk, would have been checked and mounted in its
# place. (It would not have been started: the image's check in the start-up
# file system refuses to go on while two disks carry the main partition. But
# its file system would have been read.) With this, a disk on USB gives way to
# one that is not; a disk that is the only one with its identifier is named as
# before, wherever it is.
#
# Copied into the start-up file system as well (Debian's initramfs takes the
# rules in /etc/udev/rules.d with it), where it matters most.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", OPTIONS+="link_priority=-50"
